Note



ARKK typically has a lower R² than passive ETFs—consistent with its active, concentrated strategy in disruptive-technology stocks. Wermers (2011) discusses this in the context of hedge funds (Section 6.3): funds with lower R² tend to take more idiosyncratic bets, which may or may not translate into outperformance.

Lecture 1 built the Carhart benchmark and warned that alpha is only as good as the model. Lecture 2 asked whether the alpha that survives is skill or luck. Both lectures took one thing for granted: that we already know which funds are in the sample, what kind of funds they are, and whether each fund stays the same kind of fund over time. In empirical work on mutual funds, that assumption is where many results are won or lost.
This lecture therefore studies the front end of every fund study, in three steps:

Clean the sample. The CRSP Survivor-Bias-Free US Mutual Fund Database is not a random draw of funds. Incubation, tiny funds, share classes, and mislabeled products all push estimates in predictable directions. We follow the sample filters of Dannhauser and Pontiff (2026), Doshi et al. (2015), and Back et al. (2018) and show why each filter exists.
Classify the funds. Who is an active equity fund, an index fund, or an ETF? We compare three published recipes — Doshi et al. (2015), Back et al. (2018), and Dannhauser and Pontiff (2026) — on the same data.
Study style and style drift. What is a fund’s investment style, where do style labels come from, and what happens when a fund’s style moves? The key paper is Chan et al. (2002): we report their findings, then rebuild their design on simulated funds to see what it can and cannot detect.







NoteWhat moved out of this lecture



Benchmark selection and Active Share, which the earlier draft of this lecture covered, move to a later lecture. They need the clean sample and the style infrastructure built here.


By the end of the lecture, you should be able to:

explain the unit-of-observation problem in CRSP (share class vs. fund) and aggregate share classes correctly;
explain, with a simulation, how incubation bias manufactures “skilled” managers;
apply and compare three classification recipes and diagnose their failure modes (name-parsing false positives, sector funds, hedged funds);
distinguish stated style from revealed style, and measure style drift both ways;
summarize the evidence in Chan et al. (2002) on style consistency and on who changes style, and explain why a rank-based drift measure has a noise floor and a mechanical winner-loser gap;
interactively explore the results: every figure in this handout is a highcharter chart you can hover, zoom, and toggle.

